     1	//! Port of `LongJumpToRandomPos.java` with `LongJumpMidJump.java` folded into the same goal.
     2	//!
     3	//! Vanilla splits the leap across two brain behaviors that hand off through the
     4	//! `LONG_JUMP_MID_JUMP` / `LONG_JUMP_COOLDOWN_TICKS` memories: `LongJumpToRandomPos` picks a
     5	//! landing spot, crouches for 40 ticks and launches; `LongJumpMidJump` holds the jumping pose
     6	//! until the mob lands, then plays the landing sound and arms the cooldown. Pumpkin has no
     7	//! expiring-memory primitive to route that handoff through, so both halves live in this one
     8	//! `Goal` and the two memories become plain fields. `breeze_jump.rs` merges its own flight phase
     9	//! the same way.
    10	//!
    11	//! Deliberate deviations from vanilla, all of them bounded-cost or missing-API decisions:
    12	//!
    13	//! - `pickCandidate` (`LongJumpToRandomPos.java:150-171`) loops until the candidate list is
    14	//!   empty within a single tick, computing a path per surviving candidate. That is unbounded
    15	//!   pathfinding inside one tick. Here at most `CANDIDATES_PER_TICK` candidates are examined per
    16	//!   tick, still capped overall by `FIND_JUMP_TRIES` ticks.
    17	//! - The reachability filter uses a throwaway `Navigator::default()` rather than the mob's own
    18	//!   navigator, because the mob's navigator is behind a `std::sync::Mutex` that cannot be held
    19	//!   across an await. `villager/mod.rs` does the same for the same reason; the cost is that the
    20	//!   goat's own pathfinding maluses do not apply to the reachability probe.
    21	//! - `setDiscardFriction(true)` has no equivalent in Pumpkin's movement pipeline (the same gap
    22	//!   `breeze_jump.rs` documents), so the arc loses slightly more speed to drag than vanilla's.
    23	//! - `getJumpBoostPower` is not applied to the launch vector: a Jump Boost effect does not
    24	//!   lengthen the leap here.
